<title>USA: Ashland Inc Q2 March 31 profit vs loss.</title>
<headline>Ashland Inc Q2 March 31 profit vs loss.</headline>
<dateline>ASHLAND, Ky. 1997-04-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>(In millions except per share data - unaudited)</p>
<p>					 Second Quarter    First Half</p>
<p>					1997	1996    1997     1996</p>
<p>REVENUES</p>
<p>Sales and operating revenues (including</p>
<p>  excise taxes)		 $3,226    $3,072  $6,653   $6,151</p>
<p> Other				  17	  25	37   119(A)</p>
<p>				     3,243     3,097   6,690    6,270</p>
<p> OPERATING INCOME		   52	  33     141	208</p>
<p> Income taxes			(7)	   1    (23)     (42)</p>
<p>Minority interest in</p>
<p>  earnings of subsidiaries     (5)	   -     (9)	(6)</p>
<p> NET INCOME (LOSS)		   7	 (2)	43    85(A)</p>
<p>EARNINGS (LOSS) PER SHARE</p>
<p> Primary			     .03     (.11)     .50  1.18(A)</p>
<p> Assuming full dilution(B)     .09     (.11)     .56     1.15</p>
<p>AVERAGE SHARES OUTSTANDING</p>
<p> Primary				66	  64	66	 64</p>
<p> Assuming full dilution(B)	75	  64	75	 76</p>
<p>(A) Includes gain of $73 million ($48 million, or $0.74/shr, after taxes) resulting from the settlement of bankruptcy claims.</p>
<p>(B) Ashland's $3.125 Cumulative Convertible Preferred Stock was called for redemption in March 1997, and 99% of the shares were converted into common. In computing fully-diluted earnings per share, accounting principles require the preferred shares be treated as converted as of the beginning of the periods, despite the anti-dilutive effect.  The impact of the conversion on primary earnings per share was negligible.   ((-- New York Newsdesk 212-859-1610.))</p>
</text>
